\title{LDscnR: LD-Aware Complexity Reduction and Outlier-Region Detection for Genome Scans}
\description{
Linkage-disequilibrium-aware genome scans. Estimates chromosome-wise LD decay and derives a local-LD weight for every marker, then partitions markers into LD-coherent clusters from genotypes alone, without reference to phenotype or environmental covariate, extending the linkage disequilibrium network approach of Kemppainen et al. (2015, Molecular Ecology Resources 15:1031-1045) and the complexity-reduction clustering of Li et al. (2018) \doi{10.1111/1755-0998.12893}. Each retained cluster is reduced to one coherent multilocus genotype pattern or cluster-level statistic and tested as a unit in place of every constituent marker, so that redundant markers in strong LD no longer dominate the test count or the interpretation of the rejection set. Testing is engine-agnostic: the user supplies observed and permuted association p-values from whatever method they prefer, so the same clusters can be paired with different engines and null-construction schemes. Significant clusters can subsequently be assembled into regions for reporting, a post hoc step kept distinct from the hypotheses subjected to multiple-testing correction. This approach was benchmarked against simulated truth and against an independent stickleback dataset and found to improve locus- level precision without a fixed cost to recall; earlier applications of LD clustering to this problem, including the consistency-C-score formulation of Fang et al. (2021) \doi{10.1093/molbev/msab144}, remain available in the package but are no longer the primary method.
}
